--TEST--
Core\Str::chunk counts grapheme clusters, so every piece is a string a caller can hold where str_split hands back fragments of a code point and mb_str_split halves a cluster
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
The same **unit** divergence `slice` has with `substr` and `reverse` has with `strrev`, and on this member it is the one that actually breaks a port. `Core\Str::chunk` divides on grapheme cluster boundaries (`nvs_stdlib::granularity::DEFAULT`, `rule:types/string-is-utf8`); `str_split` divides on byte boundaries and `mb_str_split` on code point boundaries. **Against `str_split`.** `"aé中👍"` is ten bytes and four clusters, so `str_split($s, 2)` answers five pieces — `61c3`, `a9e4`, `b8ad`, `f09f`, `918d` — of which not one is well-formed UTF-8, because every one of them begins or ends inside a multi-byte sequence. `Core\Str::chunk($s, 2)` answers two, `aé` and `中👍`. This is why the member exists at all rather than being a thin rename: a byte-counted split of text produces values Novis cannot represent, `rule:types/conversion` making `bytes as string` the checked conversion exactly so a truncated sequence cannot arrive by accident. The port of a program that genuinely wanted fixed-size byte runs — a wire framing, a fixed-width record — says so in the type, over `bytes`, rather than getting them by accident from a member that takes text. **Against `mb_str_split`.** Counting code points keeps each piece well-formed but still cuts a character in half: `"a👍🏽b"` is 4 code points and 3 clusters, so `mb_str_split($s, 2)` puts the thumbs-up in one piece and its skin-tone modifier at the head of the next, where `Core\Str::chunk($s, 2)` answers `a👍🏽` and `b`. `"cafe\u{0301}"` splits the same way at size 3 — `caf` and `e`, with the combining acute leading the third piece and attaching to nothing — where the member answers `caf` and the whole accented cluster. Chunking is where that matters most, because the pieces are usually rejoined with something between them: a byte- or code-point-counted chunk of text puts the separator *inside* a character. `mb_str_split` is not callable on this leg in any case — the `php` on the Windows `PATH` has no `mbstring` — which is the second reason the expectation below is frozen rather than computed. **What is not a divergence** is the rejoin: pieces concatenated with nothing between them reproduce the subject under all three units, so that invariant is asserted against PHP over an ASCII table in the companion case, `str-chunk-matches-str_split-and-chunk_split`, and restated here only to show it survives the unit change. The claim that does not survive it is the count — `Core\Str::chunk($s, 1)` answers exactly `Core\Str::length($s)` pieces, where `str_split($s, 1)` answers `strlen($s)` of them.
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Spec § 1's `chunk` row over subjects where the byte, the code point and the
// grapheme cluster are three different counts. The agreeing half — thirty
// cells against `str_split`, thirty more reproducing `chunk_split`, the
// rejoin invariance and the shared refusal of a zero size — is in
// `str-chunk-matches-str_split-and-chunk_split`.

final class Show {
    // A case cannot echo a `bytes` (`rule:types/conversion`), and the whole question
    // here is where a boundary fell inside a byte sequence, so each piece is
    // rendered as hex. A fragment `str_split` would have produced is not a
    // `string` at all and so cannot appear on either side of this comparison
    // — the hex is what makes the boundary visible without one.
    public static function bytes(string $text): string {
        return Core\Encoding::toHex($text as bytes);
    }

    // One cell: every piece of a chunking, in hex, each behind its own `|`.
    // A `.nvst` case's own helper has to be a `public static function` inside
    // a class (ADR 0011 § 1), which is what this class is for.
    public static function pieces(string $text, int $size): string {
        string $out = "";
        foreach (Core\Str::chunk($text, $size as uint) as string $piece) {
            $out = $out . "|" . Core\Encoding::toHex($piece as bytes);
        }
        return $out;
    }
}

// Three subjects, one per way the units part, and three sizes. `$mixed` has
// one code point per cluster and four different byte widths, so it separates
// the byte unit alone; `$tone` and `$combining` each hold a two-code-point
// cluster, which is where the code-point unit stops agreeing as well.
array<string> $subjects = ["aé中👍", "a👍🏽b", "cafe\u{0301}"];
array<int> $sizes = [1, 2, 3];

// Each cell is the piece count followed by every piece in hex. Read the byte
// widths across a row: they are 1, 2, 3 and 4 for `$mixed` at size 1 and 3
// and 7 at size 2, so the pieces are equal in what a reader counts and
// unequal in bytes — which is the claim, and is the opposite of what a
// fixed-size byte split guarantees. `$tone` at size 3 is one piece, because
// the subject is three clusters and ten bytes.
foreach ($subjects as string $subject) {
    foreach ($sizes as int $size) {
        echo "[", Core\Arr::count(Core\Str::chunk($subject, $size as uint)), Show::pieces($subject, $size), "]";
    }
    echo "\n";
}

// The two cells the header names, rendered rather than in hex, because both
// are precomposed and so are what an editor shows. `$combining` is not
// rendered anywhere in this file: a decomposed `e`+U+0301 and a precomposed
// U+00E9 are indistinguishable on screen and are different strings, so a
// frozen `--EXPECT--` holding the rendered form would pin whichever one the
// file happened to be saved in.
echo "[", Core\Str::join(Core\Str::chunk("aé中👍", 2), "|"), "][", Core\Str::join(Core\Str::chunk("a👍🏽b", 2), "|"), "]\n";

// Two counts, and the difference between them is the point.
//
// * **The rejoin** holds under every unit — nine of nine here, thirty of
//   thirty against PHP in the companion case — so it is not what tells the
//   three members apart, and a case that asserted only this would pass
//   against a byte-counted `chunk`.
// * **The piece count at size 1** is what does tell them apart: dividing into
//   single units answers exactly as many pieces as the subject has units.
//   Three of three here, where `str_split($s, 1)` would answer ten, ten and
//   six against lengths of four, three and four.
int $held = 0;
int $ones = 0;
int $cells = 0;
foreach ($subjects as string $text) {
    if (Core\Arr::count(Core\Str::chunk($text, 1)) == Core\Str::length($text)) {
        $ones = $ones + 1;
    }
    foreach ($sizes as int $span) {
        $cells = $cells + 1;
        if (Core\Str::join(Core\Str::chunk($text, $span as uint), "") == $text) {
            $held = $held + 1;
        }
    }
}
echo $held, " of ", $cells, ", ", $ones, " of 3\n";
--EXPECT--
[4|61|c3a9|e4b8ad|f09f918d][2|61c3a9|e4b8adf09f918d][2|61c3a9e4b8ad|f09f918d]
[3|61|f09f918df09f8fbd|62][2|61f09f918df09f8fbd|62][1|61f09f918df09f8fbd62]
[4|63|61|66|65cc81][2|6361|6665cc81][2|636166|65cc81]
[aé|中👍][a👍🏽|b]
9 of 9, 3 of 3
